\cf0 The disparity in spending indicates that while Android provides scale, iOS provides depth of monetization. For Apple, maintaining the premium nature of its user base requires a high-quality, high-engagement app ecosystem. For Google and its partners like Samsung, the sheer volume of users provides a massive dataset for advertising and service distribution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Apple: The Strategic Dependency on Third-Party Engagement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Apple is often perceived as the company most aligned with the user because it does not rely on advertising as its primary revenue source. However, a granular look at Apple\'92s 2024 and 2025 financial results reveals an increasing reliance on the "Services" segment, which is fueled directly by the time users spend in third-party apps. In fiscal year 2024, Apple\'92s Services revenue reached $96.2 billion, representing 25% of total revenue but a much higher percentage of gross profit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Services Margin Advantage\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The gross margin on Apple\'92s hardware is approximately 37.2%, whereas the margin on its Services segment is nearly double at 73.9%. This means that for every dollar Apple earns from a user browsing the App Store or paying for an iCloud subscription, it retains significantly more profit than it does from selling an iPhone.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

