\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Apple and Google
\f1\b0 : Generally do 
\f2\i not
\f1\i0  make money on physical goods sold through TikTok Shop. Apple\'92s policy explicitly states it does not charge a commission on physical products Facilitated through the app.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Samsung
\f1\b0 : Does not make money from TikTok Shop transactions unless the transaction is processed through "Samsung Checkout," which is not the case for TikTok Shop. Samsung\'92s revenue from TikTok is primarily limited to the initial pre-installation fee and the ad revenue it might generate from "Samsung Ads" shown within the TikTok environment on Galaxy devices.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Why the Apps Want the Phone to be Addictive\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 For TikTok and Meta, the smartphone is a delivery mechanism for a "slot machine" of content. TikTok's TopView ads capture the user as soon as they open the app. The objective is to keep the user in a state of "infinite scroll." Because 14% of social media marketers say TikTok offers the highest ROI, the platform is incentivized to maximize the "inventory" of attention it can sell. This inventory is created purely through user time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Mechanics of User Retention and UI Addiction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The technical "ease" with which a phone could be made less addictive is countered by the "friction" it would introduce into the revenue models of the platform owners. Research indicates that avoiding persuasive functions (like notifications or infinite scrolls) can reduce phone use by up to 37%. However, the "Digital Wellbeing" features currently provided by Apple and Google are often designed to be "not restrictive enough".\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Psychology of Engagement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Manufacturers utilize several UI/UX strategies to maintain engagement:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Micro-moments
\f1\b0 : Designing for frequent, short interactions that habituate the user to checking the device 11+ times a day.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Notification Strategy
\f1\b0 : Organizations use live activities and push notifications to drive a 4x increase in engagement.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
App Load Speeds
\f1\b0 : Every second of delay kills retention. Companies spend millions to ensure that "cold-start times" are near zero because a single crash or lag can tank credibility and reduce session length.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Grayscale Experiment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One notable intervention for reducing addictiveness is switching the interface to grayscale mode, which eliminates the "positive reinforcement" of vibrant colors and diminishes the urge to stay engaged. While this is a software-level change that would take minimal engineering effort to implement as a default "Wellbeing Mode," no major manufacturer does so. The reason is simple: a grayscale phone reduces the "ad recall" and "brand awareness" that partners like TikTok and Google rely on to justify their multi-billion dollar payments to the manufacturers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The "$100 Bill on the Sidewalk" Analysis\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The user asks whether making a less addictive phone is a "$100 bill on the sidewalk"\'97an obvious opportunity being missed. The financial analysis suggests that it is more like a "$100 bill on the sidewalk" that is actually a trap door to a multi-billion dollar pit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Opportunity Cost of Wellbeing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If Samsung or Apple were to release a "Phone that Cares" which successfully reduced user engagement by just 10%, the financial impact would be catastrophic across multiple line items:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Loss of Search Revenue
\f1\b0 : A 10% drop in search volume would directly reduce the value of the Google-Apple/Google-Samsung default placement contracts.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
App Store Commission Decline
\f1\b0 : Reduced time in apps leads to fewer in-app purchases. For Apple, which facilitated $131 billion in digital goods, a 10% drop is a $13.1 billion reduction in the transaction base, leading to a loss of approximately $2 billion to $4 billion in high-margin commission.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Ad Revenue Contraction
\f1\b0 : For Google, a 10% drop in YouTube and Search engagement would be a direct hit of over $20 billion to its top line.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Data Monetization Degradation
\f1\b0 : Less engagement means fewer data points for Samsung\'92s Customization Service, making its ad inventory less valuable to partners.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The Valuation Risk\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Investors value technology companies based on "recurring services" multiples rather than "hardware unit" multiples. Apple\'92s Services division, with its 73.9% margin, is the primary driver of its $3.7 trillion market capitalization. Any product change that threatens the growth of that segment would lead to a contraction in the company's Price-to-Earnings (P/E) ratio, potentially wiping out hundreds of billions in shareholder value.\

